course: medicine
lesson: 44
title: Basketball, tennis, skateboarding and ice skating
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Basketball scoring is simple maths: 2 points from inside the arc, 3 from beyond it, 1 for a free throw. But if your toe touches the three-point line, it's only a 2.
explain: Tennis counts in a strange old way, love, 15, 30, 40, game, and at 40-all (deuce) you need two points in a row, so close games can go on and on.
explain: Skaters fall every session, even the good ones, so the gear goes on every time: a helmet, wrist guards, knee and elbow pads. Crouch low, and roll when you fall instead of slamming down a stiff arm.
explain: On ice, bend your knees and lean a little forward, and if you go down keep your hands close so no blade passes over your fingers. Skate at a rink: never step onto a frozen pond or lake.
fact: In basketball a shot from inside the arc scores 2, from beyond the arc 3, and a free throw 1; a foot touching the three-point line makes it a 2.
fact: A full-size hoop is 3.05 m (10 feet) high; mini-basketball for younger children uses a lower hoop of about 2.60 m, and a good shot pushes up from the legs.
fact: You can't run while holding the ball (travelling), and once you stop dribbling you can't start again (double dribble); the ball goes to the other team.
fact: Basketball is played 5 a side, so 10 players are on the court.
fact: A jammed finger must never be pulled; cool it with ice wrapped in a cloth for up to about 20 minutes, show an adult, and a bent or very painful finger needs a doctor.
fact: Tennis points go 0 (love), 15, 30, 40, game; at 40-40 (deuce) you need two points in a row, and a set is usually won by the first to 6 games with 2 clear.
fact: A good serve is a chain from the legs through the hips and body to the arm; too many hard serves day after day can overload the shoulder.
fact: Wrist injuries are among the most common in skateboarding; a helmet, wrist guards, and knee and elbow pads cut the damage.
fact: Britain's Sky Brown won an Olympic bronze medal in skateboarding at 13, in Tokyo.
fact: Falling from a crouch is a shorter drop than from standing; skaters learn to roll onto the shoulder or slide on knee pads instead of putting a stiff arm out.
fact: On ice, bent knees and a forward lean keep you balanced; leaning back makes you fall more and can hit your head.
fact: Ice on ponds can be thin in places, and falling through means cold shock; RoSPA advises never going onto frozen ponds or lakes, and if someone falls in, stay off the ice and call 999.
exercise: Marcus from Chicago shoots with his toe on the three-point line. How many points? | 2 | 3 | 1 | 4
exercise: A team scores 3 threes, 2 twos and 1 free throw. Total? | 14 | 6 | 12 | 16
exercise: How high is a full-size basketball hoop? | 3.05 m | 1.05 m | 5.30 m | 2.00 m
exercise: Sophie, 9, keeps missing short of the full hoop. What helps? | A lower mini-basketball hoop and pushing up from her legs | Throwing with her arms only, harder and faster each time | A heavier ball, so it flies straighter | Standing further back for a longer run-up
exercise: Ollie catches the ball and runs three steps. What's the call? | Travelling: the ball goes to the other team | A free throw for Ollie for being fouled | Nothing, fast players may run with it for a bit | A three-point bonus for his team
exercise: How many players are on a basketball court in a game? | 10 | 5 | 11 | 22
exercise: Jaz's finger goes fat and purple after a hard pass. Her brother says yank it. What's right? | Never pull it; cool it and show an adult | Pull it hard and quickly to pop it back into place | Bend it back and forth to loosen it | Tape it to the next one and keep playing
exercise: How long at a time should ice wrapped in a cloth go on a jammed finger? | Up to about 20 minutes | About 3 hours | About 10 seconds each time | All night, while she sleeps
exercise: Ananya from Leicester leads 40–30 and loses the next point. What's the score called? | Deuce | Love | Advantage | Game
exercise: At deuce, how many points in a row do you need to win the game? | 2 | 1 | 3 | 4
exercise: A set ends 6 games to 4. How many games clear is the winner? | 2 | 10 | 4 | 6
exercise: Callum serves for an hour every day and his shoulder hurts. Where does a serve's power mostly start? | The legs and body | The wrist alone, flicked hard | The arm alone, swung fast | The tight strings of the racket
exercise: Kiri from Auckland: "Don't need a helmet for a quick sesh." Which injuries are among the most common in skating? | Wrist injuries | Injuries to the ears | Broken and bruised toes | Chipped and lost teeth
exercise: How many pieces in a full skate kit: helmet, 2 wrist guards, 2 knee pads, 2 elbow pads? | 7 | 4 | 5 | 9
exercise: Ben's board hits a stone and he flies. What's the safer way to fall? | Low, rolling onto the shoulder | Throwing a straight arm out | Landing flat on his back | Head first on the grass
exercise: Emily from Toronto is wobbly on the ice. How should she stand? | Knees bent, leaning a little forward | Legs straight and stiff, leaning back | On tiptoe with her arms up high | Feet tight together, knees locked
exercise: Evie is dared onto a frozen pond in the park. What's the rule? | Never go onto a frozen pond or lake | Fine if it has already held one person | Fine if it's been freezing all week | Fine if she goes on alone and slowly
test: Marcus: "Y'all, that's a three, no cap!" Ref holds up two fingers. Who's right? | The ref: toe on the line makes it 2 | Marcus: the line counts as outside the arc | Nobody: it's a free throw from there | Marcus, if the ball went in cleanly
test: Sophie: "This is mid, the hoop's in space." What would happen with a 2.60 m hoop? | It'd be 45 cm lower and easier for her | It'd be exactly the same for her | It'd be twice as high as the full one | She'd score 3 points every time she hit it
test: Ollie: "Ref, that's sus, I never travelled!" He ran three steps holding it. What's the call? | Travelling, ball to the other team | A foul by the ref for blowing too early | A free throw to Ollie | A jump ball
test: Jaz's brother: "Just yank it, it'll pop back." What would likely happen? | He could make the injury worse | It would heal at once | It would get longer | Nothing at all
test: Ananya: "Deuce? Proper long, man." At deuce, what's needed? | Two points in a row | The very next point, whoever wins it | Three games in a row | A tie-break straight away to settle it
test: "Me shoulder's killing me," says Callum after a week of daily serving. What's sensible? | Rest it and mix serves with other practice | Serve twice as much to toughen it up | Serve with the other arm all day | Ice it, then serve for two hours
test: Kiri: "Chur, bro, pads are just for beginners." (chur: thanks, cheers, in NZ) Is he right? | No, good skaters fall every session too | Yes, experts never fall off | Yes, pads make you clumsy and fall more | No, only helmets are needed by good skaters
test: Ben: "Bloody hell, I ate the pavement." What would have helped most? | Gear on, crouching low, rolling as he fell | Putting his hand out first | Standing up straighter | Skating faster over stones
test: Emily: "Oh, I'm gonna wipe out, eh?" She leans back to land on her bum. Good plan? | No, leaning back makes falls worse and risks her head | Yes, always lean back on ice to land safely | Yes, the bum protects the head | No, she should lean on one leg
test: Josh: "It's dreich out but the ice'll hold, go on." Someone falls through. What do you do? | Stay off the ice and call 999 | Run out onto the ice to help them climb out | Jump in after them and push them up | Wait to see if they can climb out alone
test: In a cartoon, a skater goes through the ice and pops straight back up. Real life? | Cold shock and thin ice make it an emergency | Ice never breaks under light children | Falling through ice is harmless if you're quick | You can always climb out by yourself
exam: It is given that a basketball team records three successful attempts from beyond the arc, two from within it and a single free throw. Determine the aggregate score. | Fourteen points | Twelve points | Sixteen points | Six points
exam: Select the response consistent with the rules of tennis concerning a score of forty points each. | Two consecutive points are required to win the game | The subsequent point decides the game | The game is replayed in its entirety from love | A tie-break is commenced at once to determine the game
exam: Ascertain the appropriate immediate treatment for a finger jammed by a basketball. | Cooling with wrapped ice and presentation to an adult | Vigorous traction upon the finger to restore the joint | Continued play without attention of any kind | Repeated bending of the joint
exam: Pursuant to the advice of RoSPA, identify the appropriate conduct in respect of frozen ponds and lakes. | They are never to be walked or skated upon | They may be used once tested by another | They may be used after a week of frost | They may be used if a friend is present
